<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>For the exercise above, let <m>f(n) = 2n^3 + 3n^2 + n</m>.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Expand and simplify <m>f(k+1) - f(k)</m>.</p>
<div><m>f(k+1) - f(k) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="ans1"/>  </div></li>
<li><p>Now assume that <m>f(k) = 6m</m> for some integer <m>m</m>. Complete the line below. Give your answer in terms of <m>k</m> and <m>m</m>.</p>
<div><m>f(k+1) = 6 \times \Big(</m> <fillin characters="20" name="ans2"/> <m>\Big)</m>  </div></li>
</ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Expand <m>f(k+1)</m> using <m>(k+1)^3 = k^3 + 3k^2 + 3k + 1</m> and <m>(k+1)^2 = k^2 + 2k + 1</m>:</p>
<p><me>f(k+1) = 2(k+1)^3 + 3(k+1)^2 + (k+1) = 2k^3 + 9k^2 + 13k + 6.</me></p>
<p>Subtract <m>f(k) = 2k^3 + 3k^2 + k</m>:</p>
<p><me>f(k+1) - f(k) = 6k^2 + 12k + 6 = 6(k+1)^2.</me></p></li>
<li><p>So <m>f(k+1) = f(k) + 6(k+1)^2</m>. By the induction hypothesis <m>f(k) = 6m</m>, so</p>
<p><me>f(k+1) = 6m + 6(k+1)^2 = 6\left(m + (k+1)^2\right),</me></p>
<p>and <m>m + (k+1)^2</m> is an integer. So <m>f(k+1)</m> is divisible by 6, which is the statement for <m>n = k+1</m>.</p></li>
</ol></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>6 k^2+12 k+6</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>m+{\left(k+1\right)}^2</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>